From 97cc032e3c247b5ac065eca103ed236c322b180e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Allen Gregory <allengregoryiv@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 04:34:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Native camera source: verified cameras run with no cscore
 UsbCamera (NativeCameraSource)

VisionSourceManager gives Thriftiest (1bcf:28c5) and Swyft (0bda:3035) cameras with MJPEG modes a
NativeCameraSource while native publish is on and the NAPA library is there (SPECTRUM_NATIVE_CAMERA=0 opts
out, SPECTRUM_NATIVE_CAMERA_IDS overrides the list); any other camera, or any failure, gets USBCameraSource
as before. No UsbCamera is ever made for it, so uvcvideo's streaming privileges are free for the native
capture thread (the EBUSY blocker).

NativeCameraSettables: controls and video modes read from the driver (NapaDetectorJNI.device*, a
control-only fd), named and scaled as cscore does (percentage properties, raw_*), the same mode order and
so the same saved mode indices, Thriftiest exposure range, every write read first.
NativeFrameProvider: opens the native capture in the selected mode, hands the Java pipeline the latest
fresh image at 5 fps (SPECTRUM_NATIVE_CAMERA_JAVA_FPS) or the stream rate while a stream is watched,
Rewind via RewindRecorder.forNative, health getters; calibration, a non-MJPEG mode or a failed native
open hand the device to a temporary cscore UsbCamera, closed again afterwards. The old in-cscore variant
in USBFrameProvider only runs with SPECTRUM_NATIVE_CAMERA=cscore-legacy.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
